Singapore’s tourism sector is experiencing a strong recovery with inbound tourists up 19 times compared to 2021.
(Singapore Tourism Board announces that international visitors to Singapore in 2023 will number between 12 million and 14 million, generating approximately S$18 billion to S$21 billion in tourism revenue; visitor numbers and tourism revenues will return to around 67-75% of pre-COVID levels in 2019; there is great likelihood for full recovery of the tourism sector in 2024.)
The tourism market in Singapore gradually restarted and recovered in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board show that incoming tourists will increase 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million in 2022, exceeding the Board’s earlier forecast of 4 to 6 million, and that tourists have recovered to 33 percent of their pre-epidemic levels.
From January to September 2022, Singapore’s tourism revenue totaled S$8.96 billion. It is estimated that the annual tourism revenue for 2022 will reach between S$13.8 billion and S$14.3 billion, which is 6 to 7 times higher than the S$1.89 billion in 2021. This represents a recovery to approximately 50% to 52% of the levels seen before the COVID-19 pandemic in 2019.
The Government of Singapore has taken a number of measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism sector.
In April 2022, the government announced that it would allocate around $490 million to stimulate tourism recovery.
The Tourism Board recently announced plans to allocate 110 million dollars from this budget over the next two years to launch more business and leisure activities to attract returning tourists.
To make healthy living one of Singapore &apos; s tourist highlights, the Singapore Tourism Board launched a call for interest on 23 November 2022 to develop a healthy living destination on Singapore &apos; s south coast.
Wang Ling Li, Executive Director of Sports and Wellness Tourism at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that in recent years, more and more tourists have put physical health at the top of their list of considerations.
Developing a health and wellness attraction in the southern area of Binhai, which includes the Binhai Bay Gardens and the Binhai Causeway, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism industry and economy. This will encourage tourists to spend more money in Singapore and stay for a longer period of time.
Chen Jianlong, the Director of Tourism at Singapore, stated that in the coming years, health and wellness will become an important part of Singapore's tourism offerings.
The pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
For this reason, over the past two years, the Tourism Authority has been promoting health and wellness programs to both the local public and international tourists.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board organized the first Health and Wellness Festival, offering the public more than 130 various health-related activities and experiences.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences to its visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children’s Museum, Avatar World at Marina Bay Gardens, the Central Beach Market in Sentosa, the “Exploring New Frontiers” exhibition hall at the Arts and Sciences Museum, and the newly built outdoor theater at the Night Wildlife Park, the Tourism Board will support the development and renewal of attractions such as the Bird Park at Wild Wild Life. This will help make Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
According to the plan, Singapore will develop industry standards for sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions and incentive tourism in 2023, with particular focus on waste management and carbon emissions reduction issues, further optimizing the travel environment.
The recovery of Singapore &apos; s tourism sector accelerated the recovery of the airline industry.
Data shows that as of December 18, 2022, Changi Airport has seen a strong recovery in passenger traffic, with daily passengers reaching over 150,000 and weekly numbers around 1 million, equivalent to about 75% of pre-pandemic levels, and the number of employees back at work is also up to approximately 90% of their pre-pandemic level.
Data show that Singapore has received 23.6 million passengers between January and October 2022, 42% more than before the pandemic, and 170,000 flights, 53% more than before the pandemic.
Tourism’s rapid rebound has also spurred recovery in sectors such as accommodation, transportation, dining, and shopping.
The Singaporean government and the tourism industry have high hopes for the Chinese market.
Koh Shu Dan, Assistant Director of the International Department at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that a survey by a Chinese travel platform ranked Singapore’s safety index among the top three in the world, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular among Chinese tourists.
Before the pandemic, China welcomed about 3 million visitors to Singapore for travel and tourism every year.
With China relaxing its border policy, Chinese tourists are expected to return this year to 30-60% of their 2019 levels.
Ko Su-kyung said that the Tourism Board will now closely monitor further signals released by China on outbound travel policies to respond to changing tourist demands.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms such as Ctrip and travel agencies, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in response to the new trends in travel outside of China.
Looking ahead to the tourism market in 2023, the Singapore Tourism Board is confident that the recovery of the tourism industry will be faster than expected, provided that there is no significant global economic recession that affects public incomes, and no new large-scale and deadly pandemics occur.
An introductory book for quickly understanding internet finance
This book is quite basic; it can be considered an introductory course that helps people quickly get a grasp of the overall picture of internet finance. However, for a more in-depth understanding, one still needs to follow the guidance provided and delve into the subject on their own.
Moreover, the book was published quite a while ago, and it does not mention the various measures (such as subsidies) that Alipay and WeChat have subsequently used to cultivate user habits. Nowadays, the market share of third-party payment services is probably much larger than what was stated in the book.
These days, many people just bring their mobile phones when going out to eat. Also, due to the high transaction amounts in bank statements, businesses have stopped using UnionPay.
But I’m still very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book. It’s clear that the author has at least several years of experience in observing financial developments, and is well-versed in the history of financial systems both domestically and internationally. This is why the content is so easy to understand.
It’s easy to write about grand, sweeping themes; the difficult part is to make the writing accessible and easy to understand for the general audience.
